## Proof

**Setup and relabeling.** Index the racks $0,1,\dots,11$ around the circle (mod $12$), with $A,B,C,D$ initially at racks $0,1,2,3$. A move sends a packet from rack $p$ to an empty rack $p\pm 5\pmod{12}$.

Relabel rack $p$ by $\nu(p)=5p\pmod{12}$. Since $\gcd(5,12)=1$ this is a bijection, and a move $p\to p\pm 5$ becomes $\nu\to \nu\pm 1\pmod{12}$. So in the new labels the puzzle is: **four tokens and eight holes on a $12$-cycle, where a move slides a token into an adjacent hole.**

**The invariant.** On a cycle, sliding a token into an adjacent hole cannot change the *cyclic order* of the tokens: two tokens can exchange their circular order only by one passing completely around the ring, which returns them to the same side. The initial new-labels are
$$\nu(A)=0,\quad \nu(B)=5,\quad \nu(C)=10,\quad \nu(D)=3.$$
Reading these in increasing order around the cycle gives $0,3,5,10$, i.e. tokens $A,D,B,C$. Hence the **cyclic order on the relabeled cycle is always $(A,D,B,C)$**.

**This is the only invariant.** Parametrize a configuration by the position $x_A$ of $A$ and the four gaps $g_0,g_1,g_2,g_3$ (numbers of holes between consecutive tokens $A\!\to\!D\!\to\!B\!\to\!C\!\to\!A$), with $g_i\ge 0$ and $\sum g_i=8$. Sliding $D,B,$ or $C$ transfers one unit between adjacent gaps, so all gap vectors summing to $8$ are reachable. Sliding $A$ changes $x_A$ by $\pm1$ while transferring between $g_0$ and $g_3$; combined with the gap-transfer moves one restores the gaps, so $x_A$ is freely adjustable mod $12$ (always possible since $8\ge 4$ lets us first make every gap $\ge 1$). Thus **every configuration with cyclic order $(A,D,B,C)$ is reachable**, and no other cyclic order is.

**Counting the final permutations.** The four packets must end in some block of four consecutive racks $k,k\!+\!1,k\!+\!2,k\!+\!3$. Their new-labels are
$$5k,\;5k\!+\!5,\;5k\!+\!10,\;5k\!+\!3\pmod{12}.$$
Sorting these gives the clockwise order of the four racks on the relabeled cycle; the tokens must sit there in the cyclic order $(A,D,B,C)$, which determines the assignment up to the $4$ cyclic shifts (all reachable by rotating the whole configuration). Reading the tokens at racks $k,k\!+\!1,k\!+\!2,k\!+\!3$ in order yields the reported permutation.

Computing this for each block $k=0,\dots,11$ (the "index sequence" $\bigl(\mathrm{idx}(k),\mathrm{idx}(k\!+\!1),\mathrm{idx}(k\!+\!2),\mathrm{idx}(k\!+\!3)\bigr)$, where $\mathrm{idx}$ is the rack's position in the sorted new-label order) gives only four patterns, all cyclic shifts of $(0,2,3,1)$:

| blocks $k$ | index sequence | resulting 4 permutations |
|---|---|---|
| $0,5$ | $(0,2,3,1)$ | $ABCD,\;DCAB,\;BADC,\;CDBA$ |
| $1,3,6,8,10$ | $(1,3,0,2)$ | $DCAB,\;BADC,\;CDBA,\;ABCD$ |
| $2,7,9$ | $(3,1,2,0)$ | $CDBA,\;ABCD,\;DCAB,\;BADC$ |
| $4,11$ | $(2,0,1,3)$ | $BADC,\;CDBA,\;ABCD,\;DCAB$ |

Every block produces the **same** four permutations. They are distinct, and in fact form the cyclic group $\{e,\,(1\,4\,2\,3),\,(1\,2)(3\,4),\,(1\,3\,2\,4)\}$ of order $4$.

Therefore
$$S=\{ABCD,\;DCAB,\;BADC,\;CDBA\},\qquad |S|=4.$$

